WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:03.300
FRANK DRAKE: There are so many
stars, and so many of them

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.920
are similar to the
Earth that it's

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.420
just impossible that there is
not a lot of intelligent life.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.145
And I say a lot, not
just a little bit.

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:14.020
ADITYA CHOPRA: Could
there be life elsewhere?

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:14.950
We don't yet know.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:16.580
But astrobiology is
one way to do it.

00:00:19.912 --> 00:00:21.370
MOLLY O'BEIRNE: We
use observations

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.440
from telescopes that
look at molecules

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.270
in the atmosphere of a planet.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:27.430
Which molecules are
there actually relates

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:31.492
to what kind of different
biologies are there.

00:00:31.492 --> 00:00:32.950
JAMES PARR: What
we're trying to do

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:36.250
is imagine what this broad
range of life could be.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.781
Because it's not limited
to life as we know it.

00:00:38.781 --> 00:00:40.780
RODD TALEBI: So what we're
using is Google Cloud

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:43.990
to help simulate as
much data as possible,

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:47.095
try to build from the thousands
of datasets that we have,

00:00:47.095 --> 00:00:50.860
the single observations,
to the tens of millions.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:55.040
ADITYA CHOPRA: Google Cloud
also knows how to see patterns,

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.680
how to connect the dots.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.830
It knows how to link up
different ideas together.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.560
And we want to do that
in astrobiology, perhaps

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.770
better than what a human
alone or a group of humans

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:08.250
might be able to.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.575
JAMES PARR: This is the moment
where we really discover

00:01:10.575 --> 00:01:11.700
whether we're alone or not.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.630
SETH SHOSTAK: Hi, team.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:23.300
Welcome to a live
stream here from Google

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.010
in Mountain View, California--
lovely, glamorous Mount

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.400
View, California in the
heart of the Silicon Valley--

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.117
or if not the heart, at least
somewhere behind the rib cage.

00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:33.200
We're going to talk today
about something that you

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.170
probably don't think terribly
much about, but should,

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:40.700
and that's the interaction
of the cloud and science.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.350
This may be the
future of science.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:44.660
If you're doing science,
this is maybe your future.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:48.114
In any case, I have with me
an expert on the Google Cloud

00:01:48.114 --> 00:01:49.530
and how it interacts
with science.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:52.110
Let me just tell you a couple
of sentences about myself.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:55.610
My name is Seth Shostak,
and I'm a senior astronomer

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:59.190
at the SETI Institute, also here
in Mountain View, California.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:02.130
And our job is to look for life
in space, and in particular,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.751
whether there's any
intelligent life in space.

00:02:04.751 --> 00:02:07.250
But we're going to talk about
that maybe a little bit later.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.750
At the moment, I
want to introduce

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.470
our expert, Massimo Mascaro.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:14.790
And Massimo, tell us a
little bit about yourself.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.690
MASSIMO MASCARO: Nice
to meet everyone.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:18.350
Yeah, my name is
Massimo Mascaro.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.230
I'm a technical
director here at Google.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:23.525
And actually, I work
specifically on applied AI.

00:02:23.525 --> 00:02:25.620
I have a background in physics.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.202
Actually, I was a space nerd
and an aspiring astrophysicist,

00:02:29.202 --> 00:02:31.160
but then I got into
computational neuroscience.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.190
I got kind of fulminated by
the idea of neural networks,

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:35.810
and I started working in AI.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:37.700
And I've been doing that since.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.520
Here at Google, I'm
in Google Cloud.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.020
And what I do is that I work
with some of the largest Google

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:46.730
clients helping them applying
AI inside their problems.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:49.790
So this is actually how I
came to work on this project.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:54.920
Because about in
April last year,

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.350
we started working with NASA,
in particular the Frontier

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.400
Development Lab.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.920
And at that point,
actually, I really--

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.080
my passion for space
kicked in, and I decided

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.802
to step in and work with them.

00:03:04.802 --> 00:03:05.510
SETH SHOSTAK: OK.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:06.720
Now, you talk about FDL.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.660
That's the Frontier
Development Lab.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.161
That's an initiative
of NASA, right?

00:03:10.161 --> 00:03:11.118
MASSIMO MASCARO: It is.

00:03:11.118 --> 00:03:12.800
SETH SHOSTAK: So how
does NASA interact

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:13.799
with the private sector?

00:03:13.799 --> 00:03:16.522
Because Google, after all,
is in the private sector.

00:03:16.522 --> 00:03:17.480
MASSIMO MASCARO: It is.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:21.350
So FDL is a really, really
cool program that NASA runs.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:23.780
What they do is they seek
partnership with, basically,

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:27.110
private companies to help out on
some of the biggest challenges

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:28.400
that NASA has.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.290
One of the big themes that
FDL can research about--

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.040
and in fact, I would say is one
of the most prominent ones--

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:35.870
is actually artificial
intelligence applied

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:37.490
to NASA's problem.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:39.020
So Google--

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.030
SETH SHOSTAK: Give me
an example of a problem.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.450
MASSIMO MASCARO: For example,
one of the things, actually,

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:45.590
that we worked on
this year is actually

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:47.870
is a big problem
artificial intelligence.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:50.030
How do you actually
automatically detect

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:52.190
transiting of planets
from the signals

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.140
that you get from
your space telescopes?

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:56.030
It is a problem
where, if you want,

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:59.021
machine learning is
very, very useful too.

00:03:59.021 --> 00:04:00.770
Because right now,
actually, a lot of this

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:03.680
is done actually
manually by astronomers.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.912
And actually, there is
high error rates in that.

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:06.620
SETH SHOSTAK: OK.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:10.340
So in the cloud, you have,
of course, the opportunity

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:11.120
for sharing data.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.537
Anybody can get
to it, presumably,

00:04:12.537 --> 00:04:14.495
if they're interested in
the problem of looking

00:04:14.495 --> 00:04:16.754
for planets around other
stars, just to follow up

00:04:16.754 --> 00:04:17.420
on your example.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:18.529
They can get to the data.

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:20.404
But they can get to
something else there too.

00:04:20.404 --> 00:04:22.500
They can get the tools, right?

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.530
MASSIMO MASCARO: So
I think, actually,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.630
three main things that
you can get in the cloud.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:28.421
So the first one, of
course, is the ability

00:04:28.421 --> 00:04:30.530
to get a lot of
data in and have,

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.390
actually, very, very
fast access to it.

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:34.610
That's actually
bread and butter,

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:36.270
and you have to have that.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.610
The other thing is competition.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.770
So on the cloud, you can
get a lot of computation

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.112
to run on top of that data.

00:04:42.112 --> 00:04:43.820
And that actually is
also very important.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:45.528
And this actually, by
the way, in science

00:04:45.528 --> 00:04:48.494
is not a super easy thing
to do, believe it or not.

00:04:48.494 --> 00:04:49.910
Some of the largest
supercomputers

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:51.379
today are used for science.

00:04:51.379 --> 00:04:53.420
But actually, they're not
very available to a lot

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:54.380
of scientists.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.600
And then the third
thing is in cloud,

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.150
actually, we built a
sequence of higher level

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:00.830
of things that allow
you to do things

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:03.470
from analytics and
data processing

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:05.965
to AI and machine learning.

00:05:05.965 --> 00:05:07.340
There actually
are tools that you

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.027
build on top of these two
layers of data and computation

00:05:10.027 --> 00:05:12.110
that allow you to do those
kind of things actually

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:13.200
much more easily.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:13.908
SETH SHOSTAK: OK.

00:05:13.908 --> 00:05:16.070
Now, suppose I'm a
research scientist.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:18.070
I kind of know what
they look like.

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:19.739
There a lot of them
near my office.

00:05:19.739 --> 00:05:21.030
They're sitting in their cubes.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.530
They're sitting in
their offices, whatever.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.360
And they're on a desk.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:24.590
And in the corner,
there's a stack

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.600
of reprints that only
five people in the world

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:27.836
can actually understand.

00:05:27.836 --> 00:05:29.210
And they're working
on a problem.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:31.347
Maybe that problem
is exactly this one.

00:05:31.347 --> 00:05:33.680
They want to know, are there
a lot of planets out there?

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:35.170
Are there not a lot
of planets out there?

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:35.840
OK.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.450
Now, in the past, they would
have to go get some data

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:41.600
themselves, and they
would have exclusive use

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:43.350
of that data, at
least for a while,

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:46.370
even if it was from the Hubble
Telescope or some publicly

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:48.350
owned instrument.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:50.720
And now, OK, they need
to go through those data

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:51.609
and take a look.

00:05:51.609 --> 00:05:53.150
Is there something
interesting in it?

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:54.860
They'd write some
code themselves.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:56.230
It would be proprietary.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:57.980
They would probably
spend half their lives

00:05:57.980 --> 00:05:59.990
debugging their own code.

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:01.700
And they would have
a limited dataset,

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.700
and that's how they would work.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:06.260
How is that going to happen now?

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.660
I'm sitting in my
office wondering, hey,

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:11.325
look, how am I going to beat
all those competitors out there?

00:06:11.325 --> 00:06:12.950
MASSIMO MASCARO: So
a couple of things.

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:14.805
Of course, scientific
data very often

00:06:14.805 --> 00:06:16.930
actually come from sources
like what you mentioned.

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:20.800
So for example, I can tell you
that in the work that we've

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:23.110
been doing with
FDL on exoplanets,

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:24.580
we'd be working
with Kepler's data.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:26.290
That is now actually
a public dataset.

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:29.820
But there is a delay, because
there are certain research

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:32.870
institutions that usually have
exclusiveness on those data.

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:38.100
For example, we haven't worked
yet on the newly test data.

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.640
So for those who
don't know, Kepler

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.620
is the old exoplanet
source satellite.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:42.370
SETH SHOSTAK: Old.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.140
It's been dead
for what, a month?

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:45.181
MASSIMO MASCARO: A month.

00:06:45.181 --> 00:06:48.500
But actually, it's been actually
in space for 10 years or more.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.560
And TESS is just newly launched.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.100
They launched it with
SpaceX last April.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:56.960
And TESS actually is in orbit,
and it's collecting data.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:59.880
But that data is
not available yet.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:03.070
It is available to NASA
and to some of the research

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:05.412
scientists, but
not to the public.

00:07:05.412 --> 00:07:06.620
So two things actually there.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:08.578
Of course, you have to
have access to the data.

00:07:08.578 --> 00:07:11.567
But very, very large datasets
are now becoming public.

00:07:11.567 --> 00:07:13.150
And in any case,
it's always possible,

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:16.450
even if you have private
datasets, to work with Google

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:20.600
and have it imported in a
private setup inside the cloud.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:22.600
The cloud doesn't have
to be public in that it's

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:24.183
open to everyone if
you don't want to.

00:07:24.183 --> 00:07:27.020
We have a lot of, if you
want, commercial customers

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:30.440
that have very, very sensitive
data they do not share.

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:31.300
So that's possible.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.633
But I would say that the very
exciting thing is when you

00:07:33.633 --> 00:07:35.109
work on these public datasets.

00:07:35.109 --> 00:07:37.150
And now, actually, you
have access to these data.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:38.941
But very often, having
access to the data--

00:07:38.941 --> 00:07:41.110
I can download all the
Kepler sets from NASA

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:42.364
today if I want to.

00:07:42.364 --> 00:07:44.530
But the point is that what
am I going to do with it?

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:46.390
Because first of
all, it's likely not

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:48.640
going to fit inside my computer.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.460
And even if it does, then
actually processing that data

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:54.190
requires very extensive
computational capabilities

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:55.217
that I wouldn't have.

00:07:55.217 --> 00:07:56.800
And the cloud allows
you to have those

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.860
at a very, very cheap price.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:01.620
Now, in the work, actually, with
NASA, Google sponsored that.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:03.020
So that was not an issue.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:08.660
But in general, even if somebody
had to pay it, it's actually--

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:10.540
you only pay for what
you use, actually.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:13.060
Google actually will charge
you by the second, literally.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:14.685
When you have a
machine, if you have it

00:08:14.685 --> 00:08:18.109
for 1 minute and 37 seconds to
do a particular computation,

00:08:18.109 --> 00:08:19.900
that's exactly what
you'll get charged for.

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:22.030
And so this actually is very
transformative, I think,

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:22.529
in science.

00:08:22.529 --> 00:08:24.730
Because now I have these
very large data sets

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:27.190
that are becoming more
and more available.

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:32.200
I have the ability to spawn,
with a very, very cheap price,

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.459
very large competition
to analyze the datasets.

00:08:34.459 --> 00:08:36.250
And now I have this
whole layer of toolings

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:38.833
that, for example, allow me to
create a machine learning model

00:08:38.833 --> 00:08:41.440
or to run a very
sophisticated analysis

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:43.640
that before actually
would have required

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:46.732
me to get an extra time slot
on a very big supercomputer

00:08:46.732 --> 00:08:48.190
that maybe, actually,
my department

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:49.330
doesn't have access to.

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:52.760
So this really allows
everyone to do this.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:56.605
And I would actually
put it even beyond that.

00:08:56.605 --> 00:08:58.230
There are actually
a lot of initiatives

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:01.000
right now around
citizenship science,

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.370
people that are not
officially scientists

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:05.410
starting to do real science.

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:07.090
And I think, actually,
this opens up

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:09.730
a lot the floodgates of
that to happen as well.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:11.355
SETH SHOSTAK: What
I was going to say--

00:09:11.355 --> 00:09:14.241
this sounds like taking
citizen science to the max.

00:09:14.241 --> 00:09:14.740
Right?

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:16.660
Because now everybody
can get to the data.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:19.990
But beyond that, everybody
has some very competently

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:21.787
written, presumably,
tools to allow

00:09:21.787 --> 00:09:22.870
them to look at that data.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:25.030
So they can look for
correlations, or they--

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:26.890
presumably, they
could say, OK, what

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:29.592
I want to find is some sort of
dip in the brightness of stars

00:09:29.592 --> 00:09:32.050
that tells me that that star
has some planets going around,

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:32.980
whatever it is.

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:34.860
Can you give me another
example of the kind of things?

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:35.776
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yeah.

00:09:35.776 --> 00:09:37.390
And actually, this
is another example

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:40.120
that we worked on this summer
that is also opening up

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.500
another stream of thought.

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:44.080
In the second work
we did with NASA,

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:46.030
we were working on astrobiology.

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:48.925
And one of the big problems
in astrobiology is--

00:09:48.925 --> 00:09:51.050
OK, assuming there are
planets out there-- and now,

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:53.425
we know actually that there
are many exoplanets out there

00:09:53.425 --> 00:09:54.640
outside of our solar system.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.848
We want to know actually
whether there's life or not.

00:09:56.848 --> 00:09:58.960
The problem of knowing
whether there's life or not

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.630
is to also understand
what life is.

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:03.910
There are many, many different
kinds of possible life

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:06.334
that would be conceivable.

00:10:06.334 --> 00:10:07.750
In a sense, actually,
we only have

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:10.450
one sample, what is on Earth.

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:13.690
So one way around this is
that you could simulate

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:15.070
what life could look like.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:17.310
And there are
actually various ways.

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:21.160
The thing, actually, that
we did with FDL this summer

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:23.830
was to basically simulate
metabolic pathways,

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:26.897
so different combinations of
molecules that bond chemically.

00:10:26.897 --> 00:10:28.980
SETH SHOSTAK: Well, I've
got to follow up on this.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:31.840
Because you can simulate
what extraterrestrial life

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:32.440
might be like.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:36.330
Now, look, I get called up every
third week by some producer

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:37.830
down in Hollywood,
and they're going

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:39.430
to make a film about
how aliens come

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:41.290
to Earth to destroy
downtown Chicago

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:42.749
or whatever is on their minds.

00:10:42.749 --> 00:10:45.290
And they want to know what are
the aliens going to look like.

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:48.242
Now, it sounds like you've got
the tools to figure that out.

00:10:48.242 --> 00:10:49.950
Surely, that's not
exactly what you mean.

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:50.570
MASSIMO MASCARO: No.

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:52.310
It's much less
glamorous, if you want.

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:55.030
But actually, in a sense, it's
also much more interesting.

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:58.300
A lot of what we're
thinking about life today

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:02.290
in terms of humanoid
figures and so on--

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:04.690
we can actually go
there, but it's likely--

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:06.850
it's actually going
to be very unlikely.

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:11.440
The vast majority of life that
we expect to find on exoplanets

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:15.880
may be just unicellular, or
it maybe not even cellular.

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:20.140
Maybe it would be some
kind of chemical processes

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:23.500
that go on that signal the
fact that there is actually

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:25.150
something that can
be self-reproducing.

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:25.858
SETH SHOSTAK: OK.

00:11:25.858 --> 00:11:27.100
So in other words--

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:29.512
to begin with, the whole
question of what life is--

00:11:29.512 --> 00:11:30.970
that's a different
question, right?

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:32.890
And there's actually
no good answer to that.

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:38.290
People say, oh, life reproduces,
and metabolic processes,

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:40.964
all that stuff, uses
resources, maybe moves around.

00:11:40.964 --> 00:11:42.880
But that's true of a lot
of physical phenomena

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:45.088
that are not alive, like
fire or something like that.

00:11:45.088 --> 00:11:47.920
So in fact, it's not that
you're trying to define life.

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:50.890
You're trying to say,
what chemical processes

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:55.040
might lead to something that
has the characteristics of life?

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:55.540
Right?

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:56.040
Is that it?

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:56.998
MASSIMO MASCARO: It is.

00:11:56.998 --> 00:11:59.050
And in a sense,
actually, it's actually

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:01.210
a restrictive idea of life.

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:06.310
Because there may be many, many
larger ways of intending it.

00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:08.170
Life could be
completely digital,

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:11.270
or it could be actually not even
based necessarily on chemistry.

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:14.107
But in the realm of life
that is based on chemistry,

00:12:14.107 --> 00:12:15.940
one thing that we know--
at least on Earth--

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:17.530
that life does is
that it changes

00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:20.397
the chemical
compositions of planets.

00:12:20.397 --> 00:12:22.480
In particular, it changes
the chemical composition

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:23.854
of atmospheres of planets.

00:12:23.854 --> 00:12:25.270
And the reason
this is interesting

00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:28.844
is that we have a shot at
measuring the atmospheres

00:12:28.844 --> 00:12:30.010
of some of these exoplanets.

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:32.905
Now, to be clear, today
we cannot do that yet.

00:12:32.905 --> 00:12:35.650
At least that's what my
NASA friends tell me.

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:38.740
But in the near future--

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:40.270
apparently, the
James Webb Telescope

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:41.860
may be able to do this.

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:44.600
We may be able to detect spectra
of some of these exoplanets.

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:45.640
SETH SHOSTAK: OK.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:48.640
Just to clarify for people
who don't work in this field,

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:50.890
if you were to look at
Earth-- if you were a Klingon

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:52.567
astronomer--

00:12:52.567 --> 00:12:54.650
I hope they get paid better
than the earthly kind.

00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:55.990
But if you were a
Klingon astronomer

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:58.239
and you had a big enough
telescope, sort of like James

00:12:58.239 --> 00:13:00.430
Webb, and you looked at
the Earth with that thing,

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:02.410
you might be able
to find that there's

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:04.060
oxygen in the atmosphere.

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:06.580
20% of the air in
this room is oxygen.

00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:08.420
But that oxygen comes
from photosynthesis.

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:10.200
So they would say,
oh, I don't know

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:13.360
what's there, Zork,
but all that oxygen,

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:16.180
there must be lettuce or
asparagus or something

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:17.780
that does photosynthesis.

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:18.280
OK.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:20.170
But that might not be
the only indicator.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:22.870
Maybe they don't
breathe out oxygen

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:25.450
if they're plants
on another planet.

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:26.210
Is that the idea?

00:13:26.210 --> 00:13:26.890
MASSIMO MASCARO:
That's the idea.

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:29.560
And the one thing I want to
say for people who are not

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:31.990
in this field is that you
could actually say, well,

00:13:31.990 --> 00:13:35.200
couldn't oxygen just be on
that planet for other reasons?

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:38.920
Well, we know that oxygen binds
a lot to a lot of chemicals.

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:41.410
It's very unlikely
that oxygen stays

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:43.030
in the atmosphere
for a long period,

00:13:43.030 --> 00:13:44.950
because it would
just oxidize most

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:47.340
of the stuff that is in that
planet and basically bind--

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:48.890
so basically disappear
from the atmosphere.

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:51.098
SETH SHOSTAK: It would turn
all the iron and the soil

00:13:51.098 --> 00:13:52.290
to rust, like Mars.

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:54.430
MASSIMO MASCARO: Mars
is a good example.

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:57.010
So the fact that there is
oxygen is because something

00:13:57.010 --> 00:13:58.690
is actively producing it.

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:03.250
Now, there are various ways in
which oxygen could be actively

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:06.230
produced by known life systems.

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:08.200
But we're not sure of
a lot of metabolic ways

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:09.117
in which this happens.

00:14:09.117 --> 00:14:10.616
And actually, oxygen,
to your point,

00:14:10.616 --> 00:14:11.770
is not just the only thing.

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:13.930
In fact, one of the
things they told

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:16.630
me, the astrobiologists
that we worked this year,

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:18.130
is that actually,
in a sense, oxygen

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:20.230
is almost like the
smoking gun of life.

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:23.980
Because an oxygen level like
the one that we have on Earth

00:14:23.980 --> 00:14:25.480
is almost unlikely
to be actually

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:28.112
just due to geological sources.

00:14:28.112 --> 00:14:29.570
But there are many
other chemicals.

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:33.100
For example, just looking at
the ratio of methane and oxygen

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:36.454
or methane and other
components, sulfur,

00:14:36.454 --> 00:14:37.870
there are a lot
of other chemicals

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:42.040
that could actually
manifest signatures of life

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:43.270
being on the planets.

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:46.990
But going back to cloud
and what the problem is--

00:14:46.990 --> 00:14:49.360
very often, these digital
signatures of what

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:52.270
would be the relative
components of these chemicals

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:55.600
in atmospheres, we only
know them for Earth.

00:14:55.600 --> 00:14:58.090
And if you want to know what
the signature would look

00:14:58.090 --> 00:15:03.280
like for different reasonable
metabolic pathways,

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:05.020
you would have to simulate that.

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:07.690
And this actually is
what we did with Google.

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:09.400
And one thing--
again, going back

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:14.590
to the initial discussion,
one thing that was very useful

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:17.740
is the fact that these
simulations are very expensive

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:19.510
computationally.

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:21.040
And in a sense,
actually, it would

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:23.860
take years to run
those things on even

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:27.076
very, very fancy hardware
that a normal university has.

00:15:27.076 --> 00:15:29.200
We were able to spin up,
on Google Cloud, thousands

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:31.120
of machines in parallel.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:34.300
And basically, the bulk of
the dataset that was produced

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:36.760
was produced in a matter
of a couple of weeks

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:39.390
by just actually spawning a
lot of machines inside our data

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:39.670
centers.

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:41.294
SETH SHOSTAK: I've
got to say, Massimo,

00:15:41.294 --> 00:15:43.600
because I know somebody--
well, Sara Seager

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:45.430
is her name-- she's
at MIT, and this

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:47.480
is her job is to
try and figure out,

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:50.140
OK, if we can
measure whatever is

00:15:50.140 --> 00:15:53.770
in the atmosphere, some
planet around some other star,

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:56.920
can we say that there's life
there, there's not life there?

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:58.835
Are people like that
using the Google Cloud?

00:15:58.835 --> 00:16:00.460
Are they are already
using these tools?

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:02.586
Or is this coming down the
pike in the near future?

00:16:02.586 --> 00:16:03.501
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yeah.

00:16:03.501 --> 00:16:05.060
I think, actually,
it's picking up.

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:07.540
So I know that there are a
number of research institutes

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:13.030
and scientific institutions
that are already using Google

00:16:13.030 --> 00:16:14.620
Cloud and cloud in general.

00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:16.120
I think, actually,
one of the things

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:18.910
we did at NASA today,
at least this summer,

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:21.400
and that I'm actually
really hopeful that

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:23.260
will create some
movement, is the fact

00:16:23.260 --> 00:16:25.640
that we proved how
this can be useful.

00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:30.220
Now, one other aspect of the
NASA FDL program to get clear

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:33.040
is that it's a program
that lasted about 10 weeks.

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:36.340
So there are the scientists
that come and work

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:38.290
at actually FDL here
in Mountain View.

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:42.640
And in 10 weeks, they just have
to start and end and literally

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:45.452
have to finish their
technical reports in 10 weeks.

00:16:45.452 --> 00:16:47.410
And so one other thing
I want to throw in there

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:49.330
is that it doesn't take much.

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:50.680
These guys literally arrived.

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:53.844
They had absolutely no knowledge
of the Google Cloud at all.

00:16:53.844 --> 00:16:56.260
I remember, actually, we had
actually a meeting at Google,

00:16:56.260 --> 00:16:58.630
and we were busy explaining
how to speed up machines,

00:16:58.630 --> 00:17:01.120
how to train machine
learning models.

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.068
And there were-- most
of them actually had

00:17:03.068 --> 00:17:04.359
absolutely no prior experience.

00:17:04.359 --> 00:17:06.460
And they went,
actually, in 10 weeks

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:08.953
to publish some very
interesting results.

00:17:08.953 --> 00:17:10.994
And actually, now they're
talking at conferences,

00:17:10.994 --> 00:17:12.411
they're publishing
papers on this.

00:17:12.411 --> 00:17:13.410
SETH SHOSTAK: All right.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:15.880
So what you're saying is
scientists who are already

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:17.943
doing this, or scientists
who want to do this,

00:17:17.943 --> 00:17:19.359
they're not going
to have to spend

00:17:19.359 --> 00:17:21.866
years learning how to do it.

00:17:21.866 --> 00:17:23.740
They can get trained up
in a couple of weeks,

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:25.609
and they can start
doing something useful.

00:17:25.609 --> 00:17:26.270
MASSIMO MASCARO: I think so.

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:28.186
And I think, actually,
this is transformative.

00:17:28.186 --> 00:17:31.420
Because at the end of the
day, a lot of modern science

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:34.390
is not anymore
just genius people

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:38.470
sitting down with paper and pen
and having a brilliant idea.

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:40.690
A lot actually has to
do with data analysis,

00:17:40.690 --> 00:17:42.585
with computation,
with simulation.

00:17:42.585 --> 00:17:44.710
And I think, actually,
cloud can be transformative.

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:46.570
Now, this actually-- still
we haven't touched yet

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:48.590
even into the realm of
machine learning and AI.

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:51.160
I think it's also very, very
interesting and exciting.

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:54.075
But one thing is just actually
at the basic of science,

00:17:54.075 --> 00:17:56.200
I think, actually cloud
can be very transformative.

00:17:56.200 --> 00:18:00.580
And I think it's just a matter
sometimes of culture and being

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:01.330
used to.

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:03.920
A lot of researchers today
do not know yet these tools.

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:05.680
They haven't experienced them.

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:07.570
And in a sense,
actually, I'm pretty

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:11.280
sure that as soon as they
get to know them better, then

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:13.457
they can change a
lot how they work.

00:18:13.457 --> 00:18:15.790
SETH SHOSTAK: Let me take
another specific example here.

00:18:15.790 --> 00:18:19.660
In the 1970s, NASA sent a couple
of Landers, the Viking Landers

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:24.310
to Mars looking for life on our
little ruddy buddy up there.

00:18:24.310 --> 00:18:25.859
And the answer that came back--

00:18:25.859 --> 00:18:26.900
this cost a lot of money.

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:28.399
It was like $1
billion or something.

00:18:28.399 --> 00:18:29.560
I don't remember exactly.

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:32.960
But the answer that came
back was, don't know.

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:34.870
We don't think we
found any life.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:36.880
But of course, we
can't be 100% positive.

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:38.620
Well, obviously,
the space agency

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:40.264
wasn't really happy
with this answer.

00:18:40.264 --> 00:18:42.430
And I suppose the taxpayers,
to the extent that they

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:44.206
paid attention, weren't
very happy either.

00:18:44.206 --> 00:18:45.580
Because you spent
all this money.

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:47.288
You want to know if
there's life on Mars.

00:18:47.288 --> 00:18:48.437
It comes back maybe.

00:18:48.437 --> 00:18:49.270
You don't want that.

00:18:49.270 --> 00:18:51.394
You want a definitive
answer, like 42 or something.

00:18:51.394 --> 00:18:53.170
You want a definitive answer.

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:57.190
Now, if they had had the
Google Cloud and these tools

00:18:57.190 --> 00:18:59.440
back in the mid-1970s,
do you think

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:01.900
they would have had a better
answer in the sense of a more

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:03.599
definitive answer?

00:19:03.599 --> 00:19:05.890
MASSIMO MASCARO: I don't
know, because actually, that's

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:08.770
actually whether the answer
was in the data or not.

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:11.630
So what the cloud cannot give
you is answers that are not

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:12.130
in the data.

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:17.200
That said, I think that
for a long time in science,

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:20.620
we've actually experienced
this idea that people

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:22.300
work on a particular problem.

00:19:22.300 --> 00:19:23.380
They do an experiment.

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:27.820
Let's say you send a NASA probe,
or actually, sometimes you

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:29.680
do an experiment in a lab.

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:31.330
You collect a lot of data.

00:19:31.330 --> 00:19:34.360
You process that at the
best thing which you can.

00:19:34.360 --> 00:19:38.000
And actually, you publish a
paper, maybe, and that's it.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:39.550
That's the end of that data.

00:19:39.550 --> 00:19:41.950
I think, actually, the
idea that this data has now

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:44.380
become public, and these
data sets are becoming big,

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:47.830
and that people now from
all over the scientific and

00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:51.120
non-scientific community can go
there and look at those data,

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:53.830
it means we can now start
squeezing much more information

00:19:53.830 --> 00:19:55.510
out of the same datasets.

00:19:55.510 --> 00:19:58.240
And actually, we can both
validate better our research

00:19:58.240 --> 00:19:59.695
result, and we can also--

00:19:59.695 --> 00:20:02.320
if you want to find things that
actually had gone undiscovered.

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:03.310
Case in point--

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:05.200
Kepler data.

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:07.600
Kepler has been
there for 10 years.

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:10.960
And with the help of Kepler,
NASA has been able to declare--

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:12.430
I don't remember
the number, but I

00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:15.391
think, actually, it's north
of 2,500 planets, exoplanets

00:20:15.391 --> 00:20:16.390
if I remember correctly.

00:20:16.390 --> 00:20:18.820
SETH SHOSTAK: A
little more actually.

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:19.320
A lot.

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:21.153
MASSIMO MASCARO: Last
year, actually, Google

00:20:21.153 --> 00:20:22.120
took that dataset--

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:23.880
and this is not
part of this FDL.

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:27.040
This was Google Brain, our
AI research department.

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:29.080
Took that data set,
and they basically

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:30.640
applied machine learning to it.

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:32.500
And they did discover
a number of exoplanets

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:33.625
that had gone undiscovered.

00:20:33.625 --> 00:20:35.950
In fact, they discovered
an entire exoplanet system

00:20:35.950 --> 00:20:38.470
that looks quite like the
Earth that have completely

00:20:38.470 --> 00:20:39.220
gone undiscovered.

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:43.849
Now, on one side is the ability
to have access to the data.

00:20:43.849 --> 00:20:44.890
So that's very important.

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:45.806
And I think, actually.

00:20:45.806 --> 00:20:47.497
NASA is doing an
excellent job in making

00:20:47.497 --> 00:20:48.580
all those datasets public.

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:51.700
And I think a lot of other
institutions, and even

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:54.320
university labs, should
just follow suit.

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:56.380
And some are, but even more.

00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:58.700
Every dataset, in my
opinion, in science,

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:01.450
especially if it's publicly
funded, should be public.

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:03.080
But then that's not
sufficient either,

00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:05.209
because now I have
this gigantic dataset,

00:21:05.209 --> 00:21:07.000
and there is no way,
if I don't have access

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:09.040
to the NASA supercomputer,
I can actually

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:10.760
extract information from it.

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:14.320
So the idea that now,
actually, as somebody--

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:15.760
the guys at Google
actually-- this

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:17.410
was done, actually,
with an astronomer

00:21:17.410 --> 00:21:20.430
at the University of Texas,
if I remember correctly.

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:22.170
But the other guy
actually here at Google

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:24.480
was just an AI engineer.

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:26.610
And they basically sat
down together and said,

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:28.129
OK, let's re-analyze this.

00:21:28.129 --> 00:21:29.920
And they found exoplanets
that got missing.

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:32.940
So I think, actually this is
a great opportunity for us

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:35.190
to extract more information
out of the datasets that

00:21:35.190 --> 00:21:37.650
are very often
costly we collect,

00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:40.835
and actually tools to understand
better what's sometimes

00:21:40.835 --> 00:21:41.460
actually wrong.

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:42.940
Because maybe sometimes,
actually, the problem

00:21:42.940 --> 00:21:45.240
is that an analysis that
is being done actually

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:45.990
may be wrong.

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:48.656
And the idea that other
people can look at the data

00:21:48.656 --> 00:21:50.280
actually makes it
much more verifiable.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:51.870
That is actually one of
the principle of science.

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:52.690
SETH SHOSTAK: OK.

00:21:52.690 --> 00:21:56.490
There are, coming down the pike,
some really big telescopes.

00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:59.602
We're talking about
astronomy here a lot.

00:21:59.602 --> 00:22:00.810
MASSIMO MASCARO: That's nice.

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:01.560
SETH SHOSTAK:
Well, that's right.

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:03.851
There can be telescopes that
can look at the entire sky

00:22:03.851 --> 00:22:05.910
every four days,
that sort of thing,

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:08.390
see things that we've
never seen before.

00:22:08.390 --> 00:22:10.440
There could be flashing
lights in the sky.

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:12.780
We would never see them because
the telescopes are never

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:15.130
looking in the right
place at the right time.

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:17.296
So we're going to have
instruments that can do that.

00:22:17.296 --> 00:22:19.010
We're also building
these huge telescopes

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:21.325
with mirrors that are
20, 30 meters across.

00:22:21.325 --> 00:22:23.700
And obviously, they're going
to be able to do things that

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:25.170
our current telescopes can't.

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:28.050
So the instrumentation is
certainly getting a lot better.

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:32.190
But it produces enormous
tons, mountains of data

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:33.820
that nobody can go
through anymore.

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:36.360
This is not like in the old
days when I was a kid just

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:37.710
before the Crimean War.

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:40.405
When I was a kid, you
would go to the telescope,

00:22:40.405 --> 00:22:42.780
you would put a photographic
plate at the back end of it,

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:44.670
you'd make a photograph,
and then that plate

00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:46.834
would go in your desk drawer.

00:22:46.834 --> 00:22:48.000
Nobody else could get to it.

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:49.208
Nobody else could look at it.

00:22:49.208 --> 00:22:51.480
And maybe you didn't
even look at it.

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:53.730
So obviously, that's
going to change.

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:55.430
It sounds like the
researchers are going

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:57.264
to become data scientists.

00:22:57.264 --> 00:22:59.430
MASSIMO MASCARO: I, think,
actually, more and more--

00:22:59.430 --> 00:23:01.305
I think, actually, in
part, they already are.

00:23:01.305 --> 00:23:03.210
If you think about how--

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:04.710
most of the research
I have done,

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:08.370
personally, was really at the
border between data science,

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:10.300
data analysis, and actually--

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:12.290
in my case, I was
studying neuroscience.

00:23:12.290 --> 00:23:14.290
I was trained to understand
how the brain works.

00:23:14.290 --> 00:23:17.470
But it was a lot of
heavy data analysis.

00:23:17.470 --> 00:23:21.900
But the other aspect is that
these datasets, to your point,

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:26.740
are becoming more
and more gigantic.

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:29.610
And they actually-- they are
more and more complex too.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:32.140
Because some of these
telescopes require

00:23:32.140 --> 00:23:34.140
you to do all sorts of
processing to these data,

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:35.514
and this processing
may sometimes

00:23:35.514 --> 00:23:36.640
actually change the data.

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:39.056
So sometimes, you have to do
different kinds of processes.

00:23:39.056 --> 00:23:42.060
And this actually was the
case of Kepler, for example.

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:44.040
And so the idea
that you have now

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:45.687
this vast computational
infrastructure,

00:23:45.687 --> 00:23:47.520
this vast analytic
infrastructure-- and then

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:48.510
actually, we can
talk a little bit

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:50.400
about machine learning as well,
because I think, actually,

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:52.560
that's also going to
play a huge role there.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:55.830
I think, actually, that's
going to be transformative.

00:23:55.830 --> 00:23:58.380
Now, one last thing,
actually, on this point

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:03.320
is the fact that I
think more and more,

00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:06.910
we're also starting to see data
collection happening outside

00:24:06.910 --> 00:24:12.400
of the [INAUDIBLE],,
gigantus kind of instruments

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:15.017
that, let's say, NASA or
[INAUDIBLE] actually deploys.

00:24:15.017 --> 00:24:16.600
There is an interesting
project that I

00:24:16.600 --> 00:24:19.210
got to know about
where actually, they

00:24:19.210 --> 00:24:21.640
put actually this small
telescope-- kind of almost

00:24:21.640 --> 00:24:24.660
amateurish telescopes--
basically all around the globe

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:25.990
to track these exoplanets.

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:28.600
And the idea is
that TESS will only

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:32.130
look at a certain patch of
the sky for 28 days at a time.

00:24:32.130 --> 00:24:34.070
And so maybe actually
the other telescopes

00:24:34.070 --> 00:24:36.074
will pick up from that
and start doing this.

00:24:36.074 --> 00:24:37.990
And again, this actually
[INAUDIBLE] datasets.

00:24:37.990 --> 00:24:39.364
And it becomes
really fundamental

00:24:39.364 --> 00:24:41.430
now to be able to
go across datasets--

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:45.820
not just being able to apply
the big guns of supercomputers

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:47.830
or the cloud into
particular datasets,

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:50.680
but being able to spawn between
datasets that very often have

00:24:50.680 --> 00:24:52.810
very different shapes,
forms, and they

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:56.620
need very different kind of
pre-processing and ability

00:24:56.620 --> 00:24:57.370
to parse.

00:24:57.370 --> 00:24:58.990
So that actually is also--

00:24:58.990 --> 00:25:01.420
going across datasets is going
to be very transformative.

00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:04.090
SETH SHOSTAK: Well, I have
to say, in my line of work,

00:25:04.090 --> 00:25:07.665
you're looking for a radio
signal or a light signal

00:25:07.665 --> 00:25:08.290
coming from ET.

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:09.866
And we get lots of signals.

00:25:09.866 --> 00:25:11.240
It's not like in
the movies where

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:12.906
you're sitting around
and looking bored,

00:25:12.906 --> 00:25:15.280
and suddenly you see
a spike on a screen,

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:18.010
and everybody jumps up, and
starts screaming, and calling

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:19.390
up the press, and all that.

00:25:19.390 --> 00:25:22.360
What actually happens is every
10 seconds, you get a signal.

00:25:22.360 --> 00:25:26.200
But those signals can
be very messy-looking.

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:30.730
And AI might be just the ticket
to sort through those millions

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:32.860
and millions of signals
you're getting every day

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:34.399
and decide, OK,
this is interference

00:25:34.399 --> 00:25:35.190
from the satellite.

00:25:35.190 --> 00:25:36.370
This may be ET.

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:39.580
This is not ET,
that kind of thing.

00:25:39.580 --> 00:25:42.180
Personally, this sounds like
a very attractive thing.

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:44.680
We've been talking a lot about
astronomy and space research.

00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:46.750
Can you give me another example?

00:25:46.750 --> 00:25:50.720
I'm just thinking there must be
huge datasets out there which

00:25:50.720 --> 00:25:52.300
talk about various diseases.

00:25:52.300 --> 00:25:54.790
People get a certain kind
of cancer, and this is--

00:25:54.790 --> 00:25:57.760
maybe their diets or their
lifestyle or something else,

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:59.830
and nobody has
the ability to see

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:02.440
if there's a slight
correlation with maybe eating

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:04.762
broccoli and getting
cancer of the elbows

00:26:04.762 --> 00:26:05.720
or something like that.

00:26:05.720 --> 00:26:06.595
You know what I mean?

00:26:06.595 --> 00:26:09.304
MASSIMO MASCARO: Those are
actually are big topics.

00:26:09.304 --> 00:26:10.720
You may have heard
Google actually

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:13.030
is very vested into health.

00:26:13.030 --> 00:26:15.622
And we're working a lot in it.

00:26:15.622 --> 00:26:17.080
Actually, very
recently, we created

00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:19.720
a whole division called
Google Health that

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:21.010
will work on those problems.

00:26:21.010 --> 00:26:23.350
Some of the early
results in that area

00:26:23.350 --> 00:26:27.310
are mind-boggling in that
it's not only about the fact

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:29.380
that you can now actually
infer across very, very

00:26:29.380 --> 00:26:32.380
large datasets and
understand this is better.

00:26:32.380 --> 00:26:33.830
It's the fact that
very often, you

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:36.060
then actually get
also diagnoses that

00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:37.540
are much more democratized.

00:26:37.540 --> 00:26:40.210
So to give you an
example, one of the work

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:42.910
that Google is doing is
to basically understand

00:26:42.910 --> 00:26:47.545
from images of the eye,
of your retina, whether--

00:26:47.545 --> 00:26:50.890
basically, they look
at your blood patterns

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:54.130
there and then can
infer whether you

00:26:54.130 --> 00:26:56.864
have certain kinds of diabetes.

00:26:56.864 --> 00:26:58.030
Now, this is transformative.

00:26:58.030 --> 00:27:00.055
Because if you can do
this for real-- and this

00:27:00.055 --> 00:27:01.555
is actually still
very experimental,

00:27:01.555 --> 00:27:03.820
and it still has to
be a lot verified.

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:07.150
But if you can start doing this
with a camera on a smartphone,

00:27:07.150 --> 00:27:09.700
that means that
somebody that is,

00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:12.010
let's say, in a poor
region of the world

00:27:12.010 --> 00:27:18.360
and hasn't access to super
good health systems and doctors

00:27:18.360 --> 00:27:20.530
would just be able to
point his phone at his eye

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:24.050
and know something very, very
important about his health.

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:25.192
It also, of course--

00:27:25.192 --> 00:27:27.650
even in rich countries where
we have access to health care,

00:27:27.650 --> 00:27:29.480
it means much easier prevention.

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:31.926
I think that's going to
change a lot of things.

00:27:31.926 --> 00:27:33.550
Now, going back to
the problem-- again,

00:27:33.550 --> 00:27:36.850
you can do this if you can
actually build systems that are

00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:38.710
able to very often
understand the minute--

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:46.420
very subtle kind of patterns
inside images, datasets,

00:27:46.420 --> 00:27:49.110
temporal streams, what not.

00:27:49.110 --> 00:27:51.610
So that's actually
where, very often, you

00:27:51.610 --> 00:27:54.730
need to do very extensive
machine learning

00:27:54.730 --> 00:27:55.750
on very large datasets.

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:58.480
Because in order for you
to detect those patterns,

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:02.950
you have to be able to identify
those patterns into very large

00:28:02.950 --> 00:28:04.210
datasets [INAUDIBLE] again.

00:28:04.210 --> 00:28:06.834
SETH SHOSTAK: So this brings to
mind a couple of possibilities.

00:28:06.834 --> 00:28:11.230
One is in the diagnosis realm.

00:28:11.230 --> 00:28:14.595
I am fortunate I live
here in California,

00:28:14.595 --> 00:28:16.720
and if I don't feel well,
I can go to my physician.

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:18.170
And he's seen me for years.

00:28:18.170 --> 00:28:20.260
And since I walk
into his office,

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:22.930
he already has made
some analysis of me

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:25.660
because he knows what I should
look like, that kind of thing.

00:28:25.660 --> 00:28:28.210
But I think it's
already the case--

00:28:28.210 --> 00:28:29.890
there have been
some demos of this--

00:28:29.890 --> 00:28:34.036
that if you let the AI do the
analysis or the diagnosis,

00:28:34.036 --> 00:28:35.660
that it might be a
little more accurate

00:28:35.660 --> 00:28:38.680
than the human physician
because it doesn't forget all

00:28:38.680 --> 00:28:41.980
those rare conditions that the
physician has because they've

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:43.000
never seen them.

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:44.380
So there is that.

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:46.681
And then there's the
question of-- indeed,

00:28:46.681 --> 00:28:49.180
if you're in a part of the world
where you don't have access

00:28:49.180 --> 00:28:50.596
to that kind of
health care, maybe

00:28:50.596 --> 00:28:52.870
your phone, which
allows you to maybe do

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:55.270
some of these measurements--
send that to the AI,

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:57.160
and then it diagnoses
you, and you get

00:28:57.160 --> 00:28:59.529
a better level of health care.

00:28:59.529 --> 00:29:01.820
MASSIMO MASCARO: Again, this
is still very early times,

00:29:01.820 --> 00:29:05.130
and this is actually still very
much in the domain of research

00:29:05.130 --> 00:29:06.060
and science.

00:29:06.060 --> 00:29:08.820
But I think,
actually, this is one

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:12.600
of the transformations that's
going to happen in health care.

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:15.160
I do agree with you that
very often, actually, today,

00:29:15.160 --> 00:29:15.840
health care--

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:19.950
while we have wonderful doctors,
and the health science actually

00:29:19.950 --> 00:29:22.770
has improved as we probably
could never have dreamed,

00:29:22.770 --> 00:29:25.020
even like 20, 30 years ago--

00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:27.060
I actually-- we know
that there are actually

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:29.520
a lot of differences
between doctors.

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:33.060
And the fact that a doctor is
a human person, a human being,

00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:34.620
is a factor right.

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:38.220
Your doctor may have himself
a health issue the day

00:29:38.220 --> 00:29:39.610
before he's visiting you.

00:29:39.610 --> 00:29:44.400
And maybe he's not actually
completely at his best.

00:29:44.400 --> 00:29:46.440
Or he just actually may
have a worry, because--

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:50.430
I don't know-- had an
argument with his son

00:29:50.430 --> 00:29:52.360
the night before visiting you.

00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:54.390
And again, that's
one issue with--

00:29:54.390 --> 00:29:56.560
SETH SHOSTAK: Will they
have a good bedside manner?

00:29:56.560 --> 00:29:58.470
That's a question you
probably get all the time.

00:29:58.470 --> 00:30:00.511
I like my doctor because
he jokes around with me.

00:30:00.511 --> 00:30:02.040
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yes.

00:30:02.040 --> 00:30:05.190
But actually, what I'm saying
is that machine learning is

00:30:05.190 --> 00:30:07.550
completely extracted from this.

00:30:07.550 --> 00:30:09.300
If you have a machine
learning that works,

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:12.160
it will work in a
very consistent way.

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:13.190
So that's [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:13.190 --> 00:30:14.565
But of course,
actually, we don't

00:30:14.565 --> 00:30:18.270
have yet holistically
machine learning

00:30:18.270 --> 00:30:23.127
systems that can even get
close to match current doctors.

00:30:23.127 --> 00:30:24.960
But that actually is
where a lot of research

00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:27.780
is going right now, because
it is going to transform

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:28.690
completely the field.

00:30:28.690 --> 00:30:31.170
Now, going back to
science, the part ahead

00:30:31.170 --> 00:30:36.250
of the doctor giving you a full
diagnosis being driven by AI,

00:30:36.250 --> 00:30:39.630
I think it is actually
doing a lot of research

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:42.070
into applying AI to medicine.

00:30:42.070 --> 00:30:45.130
And it goes into this discussion
we're having about science.

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:47.940
Because those AIs
always come from you

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:50.640
having churned
through a lot of data

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:53.970
and having matched
whatever the AI is--

00:30:53.970 --> 00:30:57.600
in this case predicting or
diagnosing with what you know

00:30:57.600 --> 00:30:59.060
is true.

00:30:59.060 --> 00:31:00.750
And actually, this
requires a lot

00:31:00.750 --> 00:31:03.701
of work in actual basic
science to start with.

00:31:03.701 --> 00:31:06.200
SETH SHOSTAK: We're getting
some questions from the viewers.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:07.380
And here's one.

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:09.750
Could you explain the
usage of the Google Cloud

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:13.500
tools in the context of the
NASA Frontier Development Lab

00:31:13.500 --> 00:31:18.720
for its challenges, which are
in the fields of exo and astro?

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:21.260
So you might want to explain
what exo and astro are, but--

00:31:21.260 --> 00:31:22.176
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yeah.

00:31:22.176 --> 00:31:24.540
So those were the two things,
actually, we touched on.

00:31:24.540 --> 00:31:28.500
So exo is our exoplanet
search work with FDL

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:33.390
where we were basically trying
to train machine learning

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:37.980
algorithms that would detect
exoplanet passage into the data

00:31:37.980 --> 00:31:41.700
from Kepler and then test.

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:43.950
And [INAUDIBLE],,
the astrobiology

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:46.290
work-- it really was
two streams of work.

00:31:46.290 --> 00:31:49.710
One stream was about simulating
these metabolic pathways

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:52.110
to understand what,
again, life could

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:54.030
look like on these exoplanets.

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:55.950
And then the second
part was simulating

00:31:55.950 --> 00:31:58.140
how those metabolic
pathways would

00:31:58.140 --> 00:32:01.020
affect the spectrum
that you would--

00:32:01.020 --> 00:32:03.690
the spectrum basically is
the different colors that you

00:32:03.690 --> 00:32:05.880
basically see, the different
frequencies of light

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:08.530
that you see as light passes
through the atmosphere.

00:32:08.530 --> 00:32:11.004
And when you actually
have that phenomenon,

00:32:11.004 --> 00:32:12.920
you can actually detect
what kind of chemicals

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:14.110
are inside the atmosphere.

00:32:14.110 --> 00:32:16.830
Of course, there is a lot that
goes on inside this process.

00:32:16.830 --> 00:32:20.790
And so what is in the
atmosphere doesn't correlate one

00:32:20.790 --> 00:32:23.590
to one, what the
spectrum looks like.

00:32:23.590 --> 00:32:26.880
So the second part was
really characterizing

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:29.730
this light signature
as basically a spectrum

00:32:29.730 --> 00:32:30.630
of planets.

00:32:30.630 --> 00:32:32.560
So going to the question--

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:39.810
the tools, actually, that we
use were very wide-spanning.

00:32:39.810 --> 00:32:42.255
So on one side, actually, we--

00:32:42.255 --> 00:32:44.070
as I said, actually,
we had, actually,

00:32:44.070 --> 00:32:46.662
all the data come
into Google Cloud.

00:32:46.662 --> 00:32:48.370
We actually spawned
a lot of competition.

00:32:48.370 --> 00:32:51.240
So a lot of the simulations have
been run inside Google Cloud.

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:55.020
We took some very, very
sophisticated codes

00:32:55.020 --> 00:32:57.400
that actually NASA
had developed--

00:32:57.400 --> 00:33:01.050
for example, simulating
atmospheres--

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:04.830
and we basically adapted
them to run on Google Cloud.

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:07.140
And then we run them at
very, very large scales.

00:33:07.140 --> 00:33:09.930
Then the second part is that
those machine learning models

00:33:09.930 --> 00:33:13.020
actually had to be
built. So on the machine

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:15.660
learning side, a lot
of the researchers

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:18.150
actually use
extensively something

00:33:18.150 --> 00:33:20.790
that is becoming very, very
popular in data science

00:33:20.790 --> 00:33:23.280
and in general research--

00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:25.320
the ability to write notebooks.

00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:28.290
Now, for those of you
who are not technical,

00:33:28.290 --> 00:33:29.880
a notebook is
basically a document

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:32.430
where you can basically
put code and execute in it.

00:33:32.430 --> 00:33:35.130
And you can actually
have graphics being

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:37.194
done inside that document.

00:33:37.194 --> 00:33:38.610
So we had, actually,
a system that

00:33:38.610 --> 00:33:41.760
would allow our researchers
to spawn their notebooks

00:33:41.760 --> 00:33:44.460
inside our
infrastructure and run

00:33:44.460 --> 00:33:47.520
machine learning through that.

00:33:47.520 --> 00:33:51.360
Also, Google actually
provides a number of tools

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:53.880
to do what is called
serverless training.

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:56.850
That is, you can basically
submit a piece of code

00:33:56.850 --> 00:33:58.950
to Google Cloud
and basically say,

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:01.380
these are my algorithms
that I need to train.

00:34:01.380 --> 00:34:03.220
And that's actually
where the data is.

00:34:03.220 --> 00:34:06.790
And this service that is called
Machine Learning Engine--

00:34:06.790 --> 00:34:08.659
ML Engine for friends--

00:34:08.659 --> 00:34:11.239
will basically take this code
and automatically train it

00:34:11.239 --> 00:34:13.030
by actually spawning
the necessary machines

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:13.540
on the back end.

00:34:13.540 --> 00:34:14.860
So this was very useful,
because again, you

00:34:14.860 --> 00:34:17.170
don't have to care about how
many machines I have here

00:34:17.170 --> 00:34:19.449
or there and actually
spawning up the machines

00:34:19.449 --> 00:34:21.340
with the right environment.

00:34:21.340 --> 00:34:23.650
So these actually were
the main basic tools.

00:34:23.650 --> 00:34:26.424
Now, of course, as part of
this, those researchers--

00:34:26.424 --> 00:34:28.090
and actually, this
is actually one thing

00:34:28.090 --> 00:34:30.770
I care a lot in terms of
people understanding--

00:34:30.770 --> 00:34:34.270
they've loaded into Google
Cloud a lot of open source

00:34:34.270 --> 00:34:36.880
code and scientific libraries
that they found useful

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:37.900
for their work.

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:39.460
And this actually
is the cool thing.

00:34:39.460 --> 00:34:40.739
You don't have to
think about, OK, I

00:34:40.739 --> 00:34:42.400
have to do this work
on Google Cloud,

00:34:42.400 --> 00:34:46.000
so I'm going to let go all the
tools that I'm familiar with

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:48.159
and get to learn all
these other tools.

00:34:48.159 --> 00:34:51.400
And maybe Google Cloud is
not made for astrophysics

00:34:51.400 --> 00:34:52.630
or clinical research.

00:34:52.630 --> 00:34:54.100
SETH SHOSTAK: It doesn't
involve a complete reset

00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:54.790
of your career.

00:34:54.790 --> 00:34:56.010
MASSIMO MASCARO: No, not at all.

00:34:56.010 --> 00:34:56.520
SETH SHOSTAK: OK.

00:34:56.520 --> 00:34:57.604
And then let me ask you--

00:34:57.604 --> 00:34:59.270
because obviously,
people want to know--

00:34:59.270 --> 00:35:01.390
OK, this sounds great.

00:35:01.390 --> 00:35:04.150
Can you point to some
concrete results?

00:35:04.150 --> 00:35:07.080
Has the Google Cloud
published a paper?

00:35:07.080 --> 00:35:09.250
Is the Nobel Prize
Committee calling up?

00:35:09.250 --> 00:35:11.251
Maybe the cloud is going
to get to go to Sweden.

00:35:11.251 --> 00:35:12.166
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yeah.

00:35:12.166 --> 00:35:14.680
So first of all, one thing
I actually want to make sure

00:35:14.680 --> 00:35:17.290
is clear is that these
were actually researchers

00:35:17.290 --> 00:35:18.790
working for this program.

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:21.430
And actually, they are actual
researchers that work in--

00:35:21.430 --> 00:35:22.600
very often in universities.

00:35:22.600 --> 00:35:26.830
They're usually postgraduate
students, or postdocs,

00:35:26.830 --> 00:35:29.650
or some assistant professor
that was mentoring.

00:35:29.650 --> 00:35:33.670
So as Google Cloud, where
I'm going to publish a paper,

00:35:33.670 --> 00:35:35.800
those researchers will
be-- and there are a few,

00:35:35.800 --> 00:35:38.170
actually, that are
coming down the line.

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:39.670
In terms of concrete
results, I'll

00:35:39.670 --> 00:35:41.060
mention a couple of things.

00:35:41.060 --> 00:35:45.490
The first one is that on
the exoplanet research,

00:35:45.490 --> 00:35:48.310
the team actually was
able to train a model

00:35:48.310 --> 00:35:53.020
to recognize those planets
inside the Kepler datasets.

00:35:53.020 --> 00:35:55.959
And then actually, we simulate
the dataset for tests.

00:35:55.959 --> 00:35:57.500
So again, as I said
at the beginning,

00:35:57.500 --> 00:36:00.950
we don't have yet the test data,
so we couldn't run it there.

00:36:00.950 --> 00:36:02.590
But we simulated a
dataset from TESS.

00:36:02.590 --> 00:36:05.050
NASA has a very, very
sophisticated simulation

00:36:05.050 --> 00:36:07.040
of what TESS data
would look like.

00:36:07.040 --> 00:36:09.910
And they were able to
train a model on that that,

00:36:09.910 --> 00:36:14.210
on one side, is more accurate,
at least in percentages,

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:16.450
than what we know
are the accuracies

00:36:16.450 --> 00:36:20.170
from the previous models that
have been attempted, including

00:36:20.170 --> 00:36:23.040
the one from Google AI that
I was mentioning before,

00:36:23.040 --> 00:36:25.270
and including, actually,
the human performance that

00:36:25.270 --> 00:36:28.270
is currently estimated
that is astronomer

00:36:28.270 --> 00:36:29.470
looking at that data.

00:36:29.470 --> 00:36:30.852
So the model was more accurate.

00:36:30.852 --> 00:36:33.310
And I think, actually, that
was, of course, a great result.

00:36:33.310 --> 00:36:35.980
But to me, actually even
more exciting-- the model

00:36:35.980 --> 00:36:39.100
ended up being a couple
of orders of magnitude

00:36:39.100 --> 00:36:41.970
smaller than the previous
attempts of models.

00:36:41.970 --> 00:36:43.430
That is, ti's a
very small model.

00:36:43.430 --> 00:36:44.770
It's very compact.

00:36:44.770 --> 00:36:46.300
And what this allows
is now actually

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:49.210
to consider running this in
line with data collection.

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:51.880
Because before, actually,
a lot of those models

00:36:51.880 --> 00:36:56.030
were pretty heavy, and they
required a lot of computation.

00:36:56.030 --> 00:36:58.990
So they couldn't have been
run as the data arrived.

00:36:58.990 --> 00:37:02.002
Being able to run a
model as data arrives

00:37:02.002 --> 00:37:03.460
opens up a number
of possibilities.

00:37:03.460 --> 00:37:06.610
For example, what I mentioned
before-- if there is actually

00:37:06.610 --> 00:37:08.590
a particular
detection on test data

00:37:08.590 --> 00:37:10.990
as the dataset is
getting streamed,

00:37:10.990 --> 00:37:14.320
it means you can now alert other
telescopes or other researchers

00:37:14.320 --> 00:37:15.655
about that potential detection.

00:37:15.655 --> 00:37:17.530
And that means actually
collecting other data

00:37:17.530 --> 00:37:20.710
from other sources that
could confirm or deny

00:37:20.710 --> 00:37:24.021
that particular planet, or maybe
extract other characteristics.

00:37:24.021 --> 00:37:26.020
One of the things that I
mentioned the beginning

00:37:26.020 --> 00:37:27.760
is that TESS actually
will only look

00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:30.430
at a pattern of the
sky for 28 days.

00:37:30.430 --> 00:37:31.850
One limitation of
this-- and this

00:37:31.850 --> 00:37:33.190
actually is not completely
true, because there

00:37:33.190 --> 00:37:35.930
is a patch where they look
actually for an entire year.

00:37:35.930 --> 00:37:38.770
But in those
patches, you only get

00:37:38.770 --> 00:37:41.260
to see planets that have
very, very short orbits.

00:37:41.260 --> 00:37:42.850
Because the planet
has to go around.

00:37:42.850 --> 00:37:43.720
SETH SHOSTAK: Inner planets.

00:37:43.720 --> 00:37:44.761
MASSIMO MASCARO: Exactly.

00:37:44.761 --> 00:37:47.810
And sometimes, actually,
you could see a transit.

00:37:47.810 --> 00:37:49.810
But then you don't get
to see the second transit

00:37:49.810 --> 00:37:51.936
as it comes around, because
it's taking too long.

00:37:51.936 --> 00:37:54.310
But this actually could be
great candidates, for example,

00:37:54.310 --> 00:37:59.020
for NASA to now say, hey, we
have these potential candidates

00:37:59.020 --> 00:38:00.280
of planets.

00:38:00.280 --> 00:38:03.130
We don't know, A, whether
they're real planets or not,

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:06.790
B, how long actually it will
take to retransit again.

00:38:06.790 --> 00:38:10.060
And maybe we can have, again,
citizen science, maybe,

00:38:10.060 --> 00:38:13.390
or other telescopes, or other
researchers take on that

00:38:13.390 --> 00:38:16.570
and augment the test datasets.

00:38:16.570 --> 00:38:18.850
SETH SHOSTAK: Clearly, this
is kind of revolutionary.

00:38:18.850 --> 00:38:21.940
Maybe I should excise
the words "kind of."

00:38:21.940 --> 00:38:25.730
This revolution-- it seems
that we're at the beginning.

00:38:25.730 --> 00:38:28.180
We've opened the door
to a whole new way

00:38:28.180 --> 00:38:31.610
of analyzing data and learning
things about the cosmos

00:38:31.610 --> 00:38:34.180
and about nature in general.

00:38:34.180 --> 00:38:36.490
Have we reached a tipping point?

00:38:36.490 --> 00:38:37.700
This is philosophical.

00:38:37.700 --> 00:38:40.630
But do you figure that
500 years from now, people

00:38:40.630 --> 00:38:43.300
will look back at the
beginning of the 21st century

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:45.370
and say, the most important
thing that happened

00:38:45.370 --> 00:38:49.530
then was not reality television
or anything like that?

00:38:49.530 --> 00:38:54.634
It was the beginning of AI
applied to real world problems.

00:38:54.634 --> 00:38:56.800
MASSIMO MASCARO: I think,
actually, this is really--

00:38:56.800 --> 00:39:01.520
we're at a tipping point where
this actually may happen.

00:39:01.520 --> 00:39:02.840
I could actually tell you that.

00:39:02.840 --> 00:39:06.500
Again, I'm working in AI in
a much broader, if you want,

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:09.491
spectrum of things
than science itself.

00:39:09.491 --> 00:39:12.950
AI is changing a lot
of the way in which we

00:39:12.950 --> 00:39:13.934
think about problems.

00:39:13.934 --> 00:39:15.350
And science is one
of the problems

00:39:15.350 --> 00:39:17.060
that we as humans
think of, probably

00:39:17.060 --> 00:39:18.893
one of the most important
ones, because it's

00:39:18.893 --> 00:39:22.150
about understanding our
universe and ourselves.

00:39:22.150 --> 00:39:25.550
But AI is really at a tipping
point where, if you want,

00:39:25.550 --> 00:39:28.790
it's now starting to lead
to results where we can now

00:39:28.790 --> 00:39:31.950
solve problems in a
very, very different way.

00:39:31.950 --> 00:39:35.180
Now, for science in particular,
I think going back--

00:39:35.180 --> 00:39:37.610
I don't know in 500
years, but by going back,

00:39:37.610 --> 00:39:42.650
we could really see that
starting from the '90s and then

00:39:42.650 --> 00:39:46.250
in the last two decades,
more and more, if you want,

00:39:46.250 --> 00:39:48.440
computers are being
applied to science.

00:39:48.440 --> 00:39:50.570
They go back-- the initial
computers were almost

00:39:50.570 --> 00:39:52.190
developed for science.

00:39:52.190 --> 00:39:54.380
But now, actually, it
is becoming so prominent

00:39:54.380 --> 00:39:57.110
that the idea of doing
science without computers

00:39:57.110 --> 00:39:59.690
is completely
preposterous today.

00:39:59.690 --> 00:40:02.130
And I think, actually,
in a few decades,

00:40:02.130 --> 00:40:04.760
it may be that the idea of
doing science without AI

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:08.067
would be as preposterous as
today is the idea of doing

00:40:08.067 --> 00:40:09.150
science without computers.

00:40:09.150 --> 00:40:10.899
SETH SHOSTAK: But what
about doing science

00:40:10.899 --> 00:40:12.207
without the scientists?

00:40:12.207 --> 00:40:13.040
How far can this go?

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:14.440
MASSIMO MASCARO:
So this actually

00:40:14.440 --> 00:40:15.440
is an interesting point.

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:16.981
[INAUDIBLE] actually
enacted on this.

00:40:16.981 --> 00:40:21.410
So during one of the final
technical reviews that we had

00:40:21.410 --> 00:40:24.130
with the Frontier
Development Lab, with FDL,

00:40:24.130 --> 00:40:26.600
who are actually a number
of NASA researchers

00:40:26.600 --> 00:40:28.040
and [INAUDIBLE], and we were--

00:40:28.040 --> 00:40:29.540
the teams actually
were showing some

00:40:29.540 --> 00:40:31.489
of those results made with AI.

00:40:31.489 --> 00:40:33.530
And there were actually
a lot of questions about,

00:40:33.530 --> 00:40:36.450
well, if this AI is
predicting-- so for example,

00:40:36.450 --> 00:40:39.989
there was one study about
predicting solar activity where

00:40:39.989 --> 00:40:41.780
they would actually be
able to predict much

00:40:41.780 --> 00:40:44.720
better than the physical models
that the scientists had created

00:40:44.720 --> 00:40:46.110
for that.

00:40:46.110 --> 00:40:49.257
And one question is that-- well,
this thing is predicting better

00:40:49.257 --> 00:40:50.840
than we are doing,
but it's not really

00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:53.040
explaining what is going on.

00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:55.010
And at the end of
the day, in science,

00:40:55.010 --> 00:40:56.510
we want to understand
the mechanism.

00:40:56.510 --> 00:40:59.760
We want to understand
the physics behind it.

00:40:59.760 --> 00:41:01.092
So I think, actually, the--

00:41:01.092 --> 00:41:03.050
at least in the spectrum
of what we know today,

00:41:03.050 --> 00:41:04.490
of what the AI is
doing, there is

00:41:04.490 --> 00:41:05.990
a lot of space for scientists.

00:41:05.990 --> 00:41:09.680
Because eventually, these tools
will simulate a lot of data,

00:41:09.680 --> 00:41:11.750
may actually be able
to detect patterns,

00:41:11.750 --> 00:41:15.980
may be able to, if you want,
refine datasets in ways

00:41:15.980 --> 00:41:19.490
that we're not able to do
as scientists, as humans.

00:41:19.490 --> 00:41:21.770
But at the end of the
day, we also want to--

00:41:21.770 --> 00:41:25.130
science is really about
understanding the why,

00:41:25.130 --> 00:41:27.890
really going back
to that physical law

00:41:27.890 --> 00:41:30.210
or understand the mechanism.

00:41:30.210 --> 00:41:32.840
SETH SHOSTAK: Well, you're
talking about insight here.

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:34.740
Newton didn't have
AI working for him.

00:41:34.740 --> 00:41:35.720
He had his brain.

00:41:35.720 --> 00:41:37.303
And it turned out
that was good enough

00:41:37.303 --> 00:41:41.000
to understand many of the-- but
he didn't have huge datasets.

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:43.370
He didn't-- he had
very limited datasets.

00:41:43.370 --> 00:41:46.370
Now, let take a field such as--
well, you know a lot about,

00:41:46.370 --> 00:41:48.301
and that's neuroscience.

00:41:48.301 --> 00:41:50.300
They said the most complex
thing in the universe

00:41:50.300 --> 00:41:51.870
is the human brain.

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:56.030
This may be an insult to the
Klingons, but who's to say?

00:41:56.030 --> 00:41:59.510
And I suppose that at the
very small level where

00:41:59.510 --> 00:42:03.351
you have individual neurons
and so forth, and synapses

00:42:03.351 --> 00:42:05.850
and all that stuff, that you
might understand the chemistry.

00:42:05.850 --> 00:42:08.610
You might understand the physics
of what's going on there.

00:42:08.610 --> 00:42:11.630
But that doesn't mean that you
understand how thinking works,

00:42:11.630 --> 00:42:13.140
because that's very complex.

00:42:13.140 --> 00:42:15.380
This sounds like a
perfect application

00:42:15.380 --> 00:42:16.940
of this new technology.

00:42:16.940 --> 00:42:19.820
But will it provide, indeed, the
understanding of what happens?

00:42:19.820 --> 00:42:22.879
Or does it just give you a
model, and you just input

00:42:22.879 --> 00:42:25.420
a lot of data, and now you can
tell what the next thought is?

00:42:25.420 --> 00:42:25.730
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yeah.

00:42:25.730 --> 00:42:27.900
So this goes to what
I was saying before.

00:42:27.900 --> 00:42:29.970
That is, right now,
actually, you could--

00:42:29.970 --> 00:42:32.395
there are applications of
AI, believe, in neuroscience

00:42:32.395 --> 00:42:33.770
as well where they
would actually

00:42:33.770 --> 00:42:38.120
start predicting what neurons
does or what a group of neurons

00:42:38.120 --> 00:42:38.990
does.

00:42:38.990 --> 00:42:42.390
However, it doesn't explain,
necessarily, how that works.

00:42:42.390 --> 00:42:47.080
So I think-- again, it goes
a little bit into philosophy.

00:42:47.080 --> 00:42:49.220
It's a very important
thing that today, actually,

00:42:49.220 --> 00:42:51.000
we don't get to do with AI.

00:42:51.000 --> 00:42:53.030
However, in the
context of brain,

00:42:53.030 --> 00:42:56.277
I think it's even more,
if you want, involved.

00:42:56.277 --> 00:42:58.610
Because in a sense, what we're
trying to do with the AI,

00:42:58.610 --> 00:43:02.450
in a certain sense, is to
create artificial intelligence.

00:43:02.450 --> 00:43:04.340
That's why the word comes about.

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:06.650
And one of the big
questions about the brain

00:43:06.650 --> 00:43:10.070
is what is intelligence
and how does it work.

00:43:10.070 --> 00:43:13.370
If you can actually
create alternate versions

00:43:13.370 --> 00:43:15.740
of intelligence, they
may not have anything

00:43:15.740 --> 00:43:17.480
to do with how the brain works.

00:43:17.480 --> 00:43:19.970
It still gives you a lot of
insights of what intelligence

00:43:19.970 --> 00:43:21.880
could be, m how
intelligence could work,

00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:24.380
or what are the limitations of
intelligence in certain areas

00:43:24.380 --> 00:43:25.096
or others.

00:43:25.096 --> 00:43:26.720
So I think, actually,
for neuroscience,

00:43:26.720 --> 00:43:29.300
I would say there
is the data part,

00:43:29.300 --> 00:43:31.010
there is the
prediction part that is

00:43:31.010 --> 00:43:32.690
the same as with all sciences.

00:43:32.690 --> 00:43:35.660
But in the specific problem
that you solve when you study

00:43:35.660 --> 00:43:38.360
neuroscience-- or at least
one of the specific problems--

00:43:38.360 --> 00:43:40.880
it's very, very close
to what the essence

00:43:40.880 --> 00:43:42.620
of artificial intelligence is.

00:43:42.620 --> 00:43:46.100
SETH SHOSTAK: Is that like
100 years ago, a little more

00:43:46.100 --> 00:43:49.310
now, when they were trying
to build flying machines?

00:43:49.310 --> 00:43:51.440
They tried at first
to imitate birds.

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:53.330
We'll have wings that
flap and so forth.

00:43:53.330 --> 00:43:55.442
And that turned out not
to be the right approach.

00:43:55.442 --> 00:43:56.900
Then they developed
something where

00:43:56.900 --> 00:43:58.370
the wings don't move
at all, and you just

00:43:58.370 --> 00:43:59.810
have a big propeller
in the front.

00:43:59.810 --> 00:44:00.710
That's not the way--

00:44:00.710 --> 00:44:03.739
very few of the birds in my
backyard have propellers,

00:44:03.739 --> 00:44:05.280
and yet they could
make machines that

00:44:05.280 --> 00:44:07.330
could fly pretty darn well.

00:44:07.330 --> 00:44:09.600
But in the end, that
actually gave them insights

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:11.070
into how birds fly.

00:44:11.070 --> 00:44:12.960
So are you talking about
something like that

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:15.630
where you build a machine that
can do some sort of thinking

00:44:15.630 --> 00:44:18.190
process, but it doesn't work
at all like the human brain,

00:44:18.190 --> 00:44:20.190
but that might be helpful?

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:22.080
MASSIMO MASCARO: If
you want, a jet engine,

00:44:22.080 --> 00:44:25.110
it's not very similar to
a bird, even if it has

00:44:25.110 --> 00:44:27.370
some kind of characteristics.

00:44:27.370 --> 00:44:29.790
And a helicopter is completely
different than a bird.

00:44:29.790 --> 00:44:34.417
However, the principle that
makes things fly apply.

00:44:34.417 --> 00:44:36.000
And in a sense,
actually, if you want,

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:40.230
if you go to the very
bedrock of what flying is,

00:44:40.230 --> 00:44:42.424
of how you generate
lift, you can actually

00:44:42.424 --> 00:44:44.340
learn a lot from birds,
but you can learn also

00:44:44.340 --> 00:44:46.560
a lot from helicopters
and jet engines.

00:44:46.560 --> 00:44:48.240
So the idea here
is that our brain

00:44:48.240 --> 00:44:50.260
is a way of doing intelligence.

00:44:50.260 --> 00:44:53.550
And if you build another
way of doing intelligence,

00:44:53.550 --> 00:44:55.890
maybe by completely
different means,

00:44:55.890 --> 00:44:58.350
that may actually
still give you insights

00:44:58.350 --> 00:45:00.540
about the bedrock of
what intelligence is

00:45:00.540 --> 00:45:01.750
and how intelligence works.

00:45:01.750 --> 00:45:05.100
Now, that said, I would
actually, just for the public,

00:45:05.100 --> 00:45:08.970
say that the most successful
way of doing AI today

00:45:08.970 --> 00:45:10.140
is deep learning.

00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:12.900
And it's based on this
idea of neural networks.

00:45:12.900 --> 00:45:14.760
A lot of people
that, as me, actually

00:45:14.760 --> 00:45:17.057
have studied computational
neuroscience would say, oh,

00:45:17.057 --> 00:45:19.140
neural networks are very
different from the brain.

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:19.700
And it's true.

00:45:19.700 --> 00:45:21.575
They are very, very
different from the brain.

00:45:21.575 --> 00:45:23.490
However, they have
a lot of analogies.

00:45:23.490 --> 00:45:27.150
And those analogies actually
may give us a hint about, again,

00:45:27.150 --> 00:45:30.000
that bedrock, about
what intelligence is

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:32.100
and how you can build
a system to learn.

00:45:32.100 --> 00:45:34.020
Because at the end of
the day, intelligence

00:45:34.020 --> 00:45:36.870
is a very fuzzy word.

00:45:36.870 --> 00:45:39.484
But learning is something that
we are much more familiar with.

00:45:39.484 --> 00:45:41.400
SETH SHOSTAK: Well,
Google did this experiment

00:45:41.400 --> 00:45:42.570
in-- what, 2012?

00:45:42.570 --> 00:45:43.600
I don't quite remember.

00:45:43.600 --> 00:45:46.560
But they took 16,000 processors,
whatever it was, and they

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:49.020
let it loose on the internet,
and said, go find something.

00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:50.850
And it found cats--

00:45:50.850 --> 00:45:53.600
with very high
accuracy, by the way.

00:45:53.600 --> 00:45:56.830
It found cats probably better
than any cat could find a cat.

00:45:56.830 --> 00:45:57.544
I don't know.

00:45:57.544 --> 00:45:59.460
And you can say, well,
that's not very useful.

00:45:59.460 --> 00:46:01.650
But of course, the fact
that it could see things

00:46:01.650 --> 00:46:03.660
on the internet-- so that's--

00:46:03.660 --> 00:46:05.870
I guess that's machine learning.

00:46:05.870 --> 00:46:10.497
But I think a lot of people
regard all these capabilities--

00:46:10.497 --> 00:46:12.330
the fact that if you're
a go player, a poker

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:14.280
player, or a chess
player, there's

00:46:14.280 --> 00:46:16.020
a machine that can beat you--

00:46:16.020 --> 00:46:17.884
they regard them as a threat.

00:46:17.884 --> 00:46:19.550
And should we be
looking at it that way?

00:46:19.550 --> 00:46:21.030
AI, is that going
to take my job?

00:46:21.030 --> 00:46:23.020
Is it going to
destroy my lifestyle?

00:46:23.020 --> 00:46:24.842
What's your view?

00:46:24.842 --> 00:46:26.550
MASSIMO MASCARO: My
personal view on this

00:46:26.550 --> 00:46:28.799
is that I think, actually,
this is one of the greatest

00:46:28.799 --> 00:46:30.850
opportunities of humanity.

00:46:30.850 --> 00:46:34.620
So actually, I see it with
a very optimistic take.

00:46:34.620 --> 00:46:36.150
I wouldn't work
on it if I wasn't

00:46:36.150 --> 00:46:37.770
very optimistic about it.

00:46:37.770 --> 00:46:40.380
I think, actually,
AI does have dangers

00:46:40.380 --> 00:46:42.900
and does have questions
that we have to solve,

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:46.200
both in an applicative way--

00:46:46.200 --> 00:46:49.260
so what are the constraints
where you want to apply AI--

00:46:49.260 --> 00:46:51.450
and also from a
society perspective.

00:46:51.450 --> 00:46:57.660
One thing we did at Google
is that we, very recently,

00:46:57.660 --> 00:46:59.100
this summer, came out for--

00:46:59.100 --> 00:47:01.050
we're the first company
to really do that--

00:47:01.050 --> 00:47:03.090
come up with AI principles.

00:47:03.090 --> 00:47:05.340
So we have actually
a code of conduct

00:47:05.340 --> 00:47:08.400
in terms of where we
think AI could be applied,

00:47:08.400 --> 00:47:10.500
and where it shouldn't,
and how it should

00:47:10.500 --> 00:47:12.990
be applied when it's there.

00:47:12.990 --> 00:47:17.820
Now, I personally don't think
that this kind of debate

00:47:17.820 --> 00:47:21.270
can only be in the realm of
a corporation like Google.

00:47:21.270 --> 00:47:25.620
Google, of course, has a lot
of investment and trust in AI,

00:47:25.620 --> 00:47:27.199
but it's not the
only player here.

00:47:27.199 --> 00:47:28.740
And I think, actually,
we should have

00:47:28.740 --> 00:47:30.720
more of a societal
discussion about this.

00:47:30.720 --> 00:47:32.930
SETH SHOSTAK: Is that
realistic, though?

00:47:32.930 --> 00:47:37.010
99% of the world agrees, yes, we
have to have these boundaries,

00:47:37.010 --> 00:47:37.510
whatever.

00:47:37.510 --> 00:47:39.350
But the 1% that doesn't
believe in that,

00:47:39.350 --> 00:47:41.670
or see some advantage
in themselves--

00:47:41.670 --> 00:47:43.770
it sounds like there
might be some--

00:47:43.770 --> 00:47:46.650
MASSIMO MASCARO: As the
prominent researcher

00:47:46.650 --> 00:47:51.360
Nick Bostrom pointed out, AI
is intrinsically adversarial.

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:53.497
Because to your point,
it just takes 1%

00:47:53.497 --> 00:47:55.080
to do the things
that it shouldn't do.

00:47:55.080 --> 00:47:57.570
In a sense, this is not very
dissimilar to other kinds

00:47:57.570 --> 00:47:59.886
of dangerous things that
we do, from nanotechnology

00:47:59.886 --> 00:48:02.009
to biotechnology and
things like those.

00:48:02.009 --> 00:48:04.050
But that, actually, is
why it's important to have

00:48:04.050 --> 00:48:05.130
that societal discussion.

00:48:05.130 --> 00:48:07.260
Because I don't think,
actually, any corporation--

00:48:07.260 --> 00:48:09.870
any institution
itself on its own,

00:48:09.870 --> 00:48:14.370
actually-- can really
put the right boundaries.

00:48:14.370 --> 00:48:15.270
I think it's really--

00:48:15.270 --> 00:48:18.090
it's becoming more and
more a humanity discussion.

00:48:18.090 --> 00:48:21.250
And it's interesting, because we
are in a point of our history,

00:48:21.250 --> 00:48:23.520
of our world, where
it seems, actually,

00:48:23.520 --> 00:48:26.730
we're getting more fractured
instead of getting more united

00:48:26.730 --> 00:48:29.140
as a global humanity.

00:48:29.140 --> 00:48:31.200
And I see, actually,
danger in that.

00:48:31.200 --> 00:48:33.669
On the upside, this could
be things that unite us.

00:48:33.669 --> 00:48:35.460
Because to your point,
I think the majority

00:48:35.460 --> 00:48:37.080
of the people on
Earth would agree

00:48:37.080 --> 00:48:39.660
on a number of those things.

00:48:39.660 --> 00:48:41.460
SETH SHOSTAK: Well,
finally, then, Massi,

00:48:41.460 --> 00:48:43.680
it sounds like we're at a
stage in the development

00:48:43.680 --> 00:48:46.350
of technology here
analogous to the invention

00:48:46.350 --> 00:48:48.360
of the microscope,
the telescope.

00:48:48.360 --> 00:48:51.120
These were tools that
allowed us to suddenly make

00:48:51.120 --> 00:48:53.220
big discoveries.

00:48:53.220 --> 00:48:55.550
It sounds like you're saying
that the 21st century--

00:48:55.550 --> 00:48:57.750
in the first half of
the 21st century, we

00:48:57.750 --> 00:49:01.230
might see not only the
detection of lots of planets,

00:49:01.230 --> 00:49:04.500
but life on those planets,
maybe even intelligent life

00:49:04.500 --> 00:49:05.340
on those plants.

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:07.590
And in medicine,
we might be curing

00:49:07.590 --> 00:49:10.110
all sorts of diseases
that have stymied us

00:49:10.110 --> 00:49:11.820
for hundreds of years.

00:49:11.820 --> 00:49:14.610
And you wouldn't want
to rule out the tools.

00:49:14.610 --> 00:49:16.400
You want to embrace those tools.

00:49:16.400 --> 00:49:18.540
What's your advice
to young people

00:49:18.540 --> 00:49:21.360
who are getting interested
in science and technology?

00:49:21.360 --> 00:49:22.870
MASSIMO MASCARO:
So again, I think,

00:49:22.870 --> 00:49:25.770
actually, it's an amazing time.

00:49:25.770 --> 00:49:29.040
I've always been very passionate
about science and technology.

00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:32.490
And like when I was young,
it used to be that--

00:49:32.490 --> 00:49:35.580
unless you were really lucky
to go to a good university

00:49:35.580 --> 00:49:37.980
and you have access to
libraries and journals,

00:49:37.980 --> 00:49:39.870
you can't really
learn a lot of things.

00:49:39.870 --> 00:49:43.650
Nowadays, young people
can go on YouTube

00:49:43.650 --> 00:49:47.490
and watch like lessons of the
best professors in the best

00:49:47.490 --> 00:49:50.370
universities for free.

00:49:50.370 --> 00:49:52.290
And this actually
is great content.

00:49:52.290 --> 00:49:54.580
You guys can play with
things, as I said.

00:49:54.580 --> 00:49:56.124
It doesn't take a lot of money.

00:49:56.124 --> 00:49:58.290
And by the way, there are
also educational programs.

00:49:58.290 --> 00:50:00.510
If you guys are
interested, you can ping us

00:50:00.510 --> 00:50:03.277
at Google Cloud where we can
actually give you credits.

00:50:03.277 --> 00:50:04.860
But what I'm saying
is that it doesn't

00:50:04.860 --> 00:50:08.035
take much to get-- and even
do serious science if you

00:50:08.035 --> 00:50:08.910
are interested in it.

00:50:08.910 --> 00:50:10.951
And I think, actually,
this actually democratizes

00:50:10.951 --> 00:50:12.030
science enormously.

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:14.196
And I'm really hoping that
for the young people that

00:50:14.196 --> 00:50:15.810
are listening to
us, they'll actually

00:50:15.810 --> 00:50:19.020
get actually the passion for
science in the right way.

00:50:19.020 --> 00:50:23.160
And now they have all the tools,
literally inside their laptop,

00:50:23.160 --> 00:50:25.054
to go and do seriously it.

00:50:25.054 --> 00:50:26.970
And I think, actually,
this is transformative.

00:50:26.970 --> 00:50:29.700
This actually enlarges
the targeted population

00:50:29.700 --> 00:50:32.550
for scientists by a number
of order of magnitude

00:50:32.550 --> 00:50:34.620
from what it used to be.

00:50:34.620 --> 00:50:37.160
SETH SHOSTAK: It sounds like
we're opening doors, big doors.

00:50:37.160 --> 00:50:37.680
MASSIMO MASCARO: Yeah.

00:50:37.680 --> 00:50:39.900
SETH SHOSTAK: I think people
will have the motivation

00:50:39.900 --> 00:50:41.030
to walk through.

00:50:41.030 --> 00:50:44.310
Massimo Mascaro, thanks so
very much for speaking with us.

00:50:44.310 --> 00:50:45.260
I'm Seth Shostak.

00:50:45.260 --> 00:50:47.720
Thank you for tuning in.

